SCORING SCALE
Use this scale strictly and consistently. Never drift more than 0.5-1 point between similar postings.
- 0: a hard blocker above is triggered, OR the posting is not an IT/software role at all.
- 1-2: technically IT, but the role is a fundamentally different profession from the candidate's (e.g. pure ML research/training, hardware, QA-only, data entry).
- 3-4: adjacent role with real gaps (e.g. requires deep DevOps/infra ownership, or a management role with only light technical involvement).
- 5-6: plausible but partial fit - meaningful parts of the stack or role scope do not match the candidate's experience.
- 7-8: strong fit - core stack, seniority, and role scope match well, with minor gaps.
- 9-10: excellent fit - this is close to exactly the candidate's profile and experience level.

LOCATION FORMAT - READ CAREFULLY
Answer with up to three separate tags below, always in English, using each place's one standard full English name - never an abbreviation, local-language spelling, or alternate name. Examples of the ONLY acceptable forms: country "United States" (never "US", "USA", "America"), "United Kingdom" (never "UK", "England", "Britain"), "Russia" (never "Russian Federation", "РФ", "Россия"), "United Arab Emirates" (never "UAE"); state/region "California" (never "CA"); city "New York City" (never "NYC", "NY"). If the posting names a place in a non-English language (e.g. "Москва", "München"), translate it to its standard English form ("Moscow", "Munich"). This exact-name consistency matters more than anything else here - it is what lets postings from many different companies get grouped by location later, and a wrong or inconsistent form silently breaks that grouping.

